Supplemental Figure 5 - Statistically significant (FDR adjusted p &lt; .05) differences in means in z-score distributions. Difference in means is calculated as mean of the z-score distribution of the phenotype listed on the right (rows) minus the mean of the z-score distribution of phenotype on the top (columns). For example, the top row are the differences in means of the z-score distributions for each phenotype relative to the controls, which is identical to the primary analysis seen in Figures 5 and 6. A positive value indicates that the mean of the z-score distribution of the phenotype at the top is smaller than the mean of the z-score distribution of the phenotype at the right. LI - Laterality Index, calculated as (L-R)/(L+R), where L is the volume of the left region and R is the volume of the right region. A positive value in the difference in means in the laterality index indicates that volumes of the left sided region are smaller than the volumes of the right sided region between the two phenotypes. Similarly, a negative value in the laterality index indicates the volumes of the right sided region are smaller than the left. High resolution version available.

